course: dictionary
lesson: 749
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. Batter is a good example. Ask yourself what the sentence is about: cooking, sport or the weather.
explain: «Whisk the batter until smooth» is about cooking, so batter means a runny mix of flour, eggs and milk. «In baseball and softball the batter wears a helmet» is about sport, so batter means the player who hits the ball. «Storms batter the coast» is about force, so batter means to hit hard again and again.
explain: Look for clue words. Whisk, pancakes and fish point to food. Helmet, ball and home plate point to sport. Storms and coast point to hard hitting. When you have a meaning, put it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: Batter can be a noun (a thick mixture, or a player) or a verb (to hit again and again).
fact: «Batter» as a mixture: pancake batter, cake batter, and battered fish, which is fish coated in batter before it is fried.
fact: «Batter» as a player: in baseball the batter stands at the plate and tries to hit the ball. In cricket, batter and batsman both mean the player who hits.
fact: «Battered» means hit hard again and again, as in «waves battered the boat».
fact: The same spelling can mean very different things, so always check the clue words around it.
exercise: «Whisk the batter until smooth before you pour it into the pan.» What does batter mean here? | A player who hits the ball | *A runny mixture of flour, eggs and milk | To hit hard again and again | A wooden spoon for stirring
exercise: «In baseball the batter stands at home plate and waits for the ball.» Who is the batter? | A cook who makes cakes | The person who throws the ball | *The player who tries to hit the ball | A referee
exercise: «Storms batter the coast every winter.» What does batter mean here? | To wash with warm water | *To hit hard again and again | To cook in a bowl | To stand still and watch
test: «Fish in batter is crispy when it is fried.» What does batter mean here? | To hit again and again | A player in a game | A large bowl for mixing | *A runny mix of flour, eggs and milk, used to coat food
test: «The battered boat limped back to the harbour after the storm.» What does battered mean? | Newly built | Painted bright red | Very quiet | *Hit hard again and again, so damaged
exam: In «The batter hit the ball over the fence» and «Pour the batter into the tin», what tells you which meaning is which? | The capital letter at the start | *The clue words: ball and fence point to sport, tin and pour point to cooking | Nothing, the sentences are the same | The number of letters in the word
